[Term]
id: CL:0020014
name: intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cell
def: "A retinal ganglion cell that is intrinsically photosensitive, functioning as a non-image-forming photoreceptor, distinguished from rod and cone photoreceptors by the expression of the photopigment melanopsin in mice and humans (Hattar et al., 2002; Tri Hoang Do & Yau, 2015). Located primarily in the ganglion cell layer with some displaced somata in the inner nuclear layer, this neuron depolarises directly in response to environmental irradiance (Tri Hoang Do & Yau, 2015). It projects via the retinohypothalamic tract to central targets, including the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) for circadian photoentrainment and the olivary pretectal nucleus (OPN) for the pupillary light reflex." [PMID:11834834, PMID:20959623, Wikipedia:Intrinsically_photosensitive_retinal_ganglion_cell]
