[Term]
id: UBERON:0005488
name: superior mesenteric plexus
def: "The superior mesenteric plexus is a continuation of the lower part of the celiac plexus, receiving a branch from the junction of the right vagus nerve with the plexus. It surrounds the superior mesenteric artery, accompanies it into the mesentery, and divides into a number of secondary plexuses, which are distributed to all the parts supplied by the artery, viz. , pancreatic branches to the pancreas; intestinal branches to the small intestine; and ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches, which supply the corresponding parts of the great intestine. The nerves composing this plexus are white in color and firm in texture; in the upper part of the plexus close to the origin of the superior mesenteric artery is the superior mesenteric ganglion." [Wikipedia:Superior_mesenteric_plexus]
subset: human_reference_atlas
synonym: "plexus mesentericus superior" EXACT OMO:0003011 [Wikipedia:Superior_mesenteric_plexus]
